    "id": "train-1756",
    "question": "Madeline needs $1200 to pay rent, $400 for groceries, $200 for medical expenses, and $60 for utilities. She also wants to save $200 in case of an emergency. She makes $15 per hour at her job. How many hours will Madeline need to work this month?",
    "gold": "138",
    "answer": "Madeline needs 1200+400+200+60+200 = $<<1200+400+200+60+200=2060>>2060 to pay all of her bills.\nShe will need to work $2060/$15 per hour = 137.33 hours.\nShe will need to work 138 hours to pay all of her bills\n#### 138"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many hours Madeline needs to work this month, we first need to calculate her total expenses and savings. Then, we will subtract her total expenses from her total income to find out how much more she needs to earn. Finally, we will divide the remaining amount by her hourly wage to find out how many hours she needs to work.\n\n1. Calculate Madeline's total expenses:\n   - Rent: $1200\n   - Groceries: $400\n   - Medical expenses: $200\n   - Utilities: $60\n   - Emergency savings: $200\n\n   Adding these together:\n   \\[\n   1200 + 400 + 200 + 60 + 200 = 2060\n   \\]\n\n2. Determine how much more Madeline needs to earn:\n   Madeline needs a total of $2060 to cover her expenses and savings. Since she already has $1500 from her previous work, she needs to earn:\n   \\[\n   2060 - 1500 = 560\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the number of hours Madeline needs to work to earn $560:\n   Madeline earns $15 per hour. To find out how many hours she needs to work, we divide her total earnings needed by her hourly wage:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{560}{15} = \\frac{560 \\div 5}{15 \\div 5} = \\frac{112}{3} \\approx 37.33\n   \\]\n\nSince Madeline cannot work a fraction of an hour in most practical scenarios, we round up to the next whole number. Therefore, Madeline needs to work 38 hours this month.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{38}\n\\]",
